"Where can you see the real writing of the masters in this series? One answer is Taipei. The National Palace Museum there keeps more than 3,700 works of calligraphy, and some of them are among the most famous in the world.": "61ef4b07bfcd",
"Where can you see the real writing of the masters in this series? One answer is Taipei. The National Palace Museum there keeps more than 3,700 works of calligraphy, and some of them are among the most famous in the world. The museum began in 1925 in the old palace in Beijing. In 1933, as war came closer, more than thirteen thousand crates of its treasures were sent south for safety. In 1948 and 1949, a selection of the finest pieces was shipped to Taiwan, and in 1965 a new museum building opened in Taipei. This lesson looks at five works. Two were written by people you have already met: a short letter by Wang Xizhi, kept as a fine Tang dynasty copy, and Yan Zhenqing's draft for his nephew. Two are in cursive script: Sun Guoting's long scroll about how to write, and the wild cursive of the monk Huaisu. The last is by the Song dynasty poet Su Shi. In 1080 Su Shi was sent away from the capital to live in Huangzhou. During his third Cold Food Festival there, in 1082, it rained and rained, and he wrote two sad poems. On the scroll, the characters begin small and grow larger and heavier as his feelings rise. The lower edge of the scroll is marked by fire. It was once taken to Japan, and the museum bought it in 1987. You cannot see these works every day. Paper and silk are fragile, and light harms them a little at a time. The harm adds up and cannot be undone. So the museum keeps paintings and calligraphy at no more than 50 lux, which is far dimmer than daylight. Its most protected works may be shown for no more than 42 days, and then they rest for at least three years. All five are National Treasures. After ten lessons, you can read them in a new way: you know the scripts, the strokes, and how a brush makes thick and thin lines. If you visit, walk slowly, look closely, and never use a flash.": "a68babc24e9e",
"Why does the museum show its most protected works for only a short time?": "4b15dda827fe",
